28467,0.009284973,0.0013256073,-0.039245605,0.0049972534,0.0068740845,-0.02267456,-0.04269409,0.01852417,-0.001906395,-0.008003235,-0.0040016174,-0.0028209686,0.0057792664,0.013679504,0.031341553,0.024368286,-0.0028095245,0.011749268,0.016052246,0.04421997,-0.0016479492,0.0015354156,0.008712769,0.014015198,0.021697998,0.04727173,-0.0073242188,0.049743652,0.0011405945,-0.028564453,0.02154541,0.013023376,-0.00043678284,-0.04208374,0.02822876,0.016036987,-0.0099105835,-0.0158844,-0.040222168,0.0055885315,0.008453369,-7.7188015e-05,0.007713318,-0.003452301,0.041137695,-0.0032196045,-0.021896362,0.013618469,0.03265381,-0.025970459,0.017440796,-0.013137817,0.039886475,0.0022335052,0.012863159,0.0031414032,-0.004863739,0.0073242188,0.028793335,-0.0025596619,-0.0112838745,0.0015172958,-0.02947998,0.00083112717,0.016693115,0.03656006,0.016693115,0.02407837,0.0011558533,0.015991211,0.028411865,0.0066452026,-0.01159668,0.0104904175,-0.019439697,0.002500534,0.011497498,-0.027694702,-0.007434845,0.018875122,-0.026382446,-0.021759033,-0.004650116,-0.011741638,-0.022567749,0.036132812,0.00894928,0.011413574,-0.006336212,0.006980896,-0.026321411,0.005542755,0.003753662,-0.0045051575,-0.017333984,-0.011131287,0.00022470951,-0.017501831,0.0023174286,-0.004688263,0.02722168,-0.028030396,0.02217102,0.010299683,-0.001964569,-0.0066947937,-0.02558899,-0.008659363,-0.02798462]'::public.vector,'title:Stopping AI-Generated Architectural Drift: Deterministic Guardrails for Agentic Coding, description:Agentic coding generates code faster than any team can review it, and that speed lets a single questionable pattern spread across a codebase before anyone notices. This talk shows how to stop that drift at the source, with layered guardrails that make architectural violations structurally hard to introduce instead of something you catch too late in review.

Drawn from reviewing hundreds of AI-assisted pull requests: natural-language (NL) repo rules don''t hold the line against an LLM. The reliable fix is a deterministic foundation, executable architectural rules acting as fitness functions for AI-generated code, built with ArchUnit, custom linters, and CI gates that fail the build the instant a boundary is crossed. That layer is also what makes NL rules work: once it fences off the cases that must never be wrong, targeted NL rules become precise instead of suggestions an agent ignores. Includes live demos of guardrails catching violations in real time.

Takeaways: why NL rules fail to stop LLMs propagating bad patterns; how to build a deterministic enforcement layer (ArchUnit, custom linters, CI gates) with instant feedback; how that layer makes your NL rules reliable; a practical model for sorting any rule into the right layer.

Audience: software architects, tech leads, and senior engineers maintaining architectural consistency on AI-augmented teams.','{"id": "accc49c81aad4956bce675837efd5450", "room": "TBA 3", "title": "Stopping AI-Generated Architectural Drift: Deterministic Guardrails for Agentic Coding", "endsAt": 1791216300000, "category": "[Tools-in-Action, INTERMEDIATE, Architecture & Modernization]", "dayIndex": 1, "speakers": "[Sasha Podlesniuk]", "startsAt": 1791214500000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-10-05", "durationMinutes": 30, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-05T18:05:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-05T17:35:00"}'),
