.022262573,-0.034942627,-0.0076904297,-0.04019165,-0.034942627,0.01637268,0.016464233,-0.029525757,0.023040771,-0.008842468,-0.003540039,-0.004512787,0.035217285,0.048461914,0.014190674,0.012809753,-0.0030593872,-0.009048462,0.007980347,-0.021881104,0.024795532,0.02079773,0.007980347,0.03427124,-0.0009813309,0.016586304,-0.01309967,-0.019210815,0.0090408325,0.005039215,-0.017333984,-0.009925842,0.020950317,0.027359009,0.0012989044,-0.016113281,-0.039276123,0.02633667,0.014022827,-0.0016717911,0.015350342,-0.011001587,0.049346924,-0.006778717,-0.016082764,0.014884949,0.028320312,-0.0016145706,0.028793335,0.0012922287,0.02458191,-0.016921997,0.03765869,-0.005508423,-0.011184692,-0.0017213821,0.014137268,-0.0029697418,-0.006313324,-0.008216858,-0.0041542053,-0.0027770996,-0.017654419,0.04034424,0.011154175,0.011833191,-0.019195557,0.024459839,-0.015449524,-0.0061683655,-0.0025482178,0.041229248,-0.0069389343,-0.001627922,0.0075531006,-0.012054443,-0.014350891,0.026062012,-0.02848816,-0.02104187,-0.02067566,0.0053710938,-0.02897644,0.052642822,0.016647339,0.0024280548,0.039367676,0.036254883,0.008178711,-0.031051636,0.006477356,-0.019439697,-0.028930664,0.008804321,-0.030380249,0.014587402,-0.019958496,-0.019180298,0.023330688,-0.034362793,0.020904541,0.044677734,-0.011940002,-0.01966858,-0.05343628,-0.0015592575,-0.018859863]'::public.vector,'title:Build the Builder: A Java Coding Agent in Your IDE with ACP, description:Most agent talks stop when the agent can call a tool. This session tackles the harder and more practical question: how do you build a custom, production-minded agent for your organization’s workflows and guardrails and make it a first-class participant inside the developer’s IDE?

In this live-coded session, we’ll build a coding agent in Java / Kotlin, connect it directly to an IntelliJ-based IDE through the Agent Client Protocol (ACP), and extend it into an orchestrator that can collaborate with external coding agents such as Codex, Claude, and Gemini.

You’ll learn how to:

Build your own agent on the JVM, including its reasoning loop, tools, state, and guardrails. Although we’ll create a coding agent, the same techniques apply to chatbots, banking assistants, support agents, and other domain-specific systems.

Connect a custom agent to an IDE through ACP protocol, without creating a dedicated integration for every agent.

Integrate and orchestrate multiple external coding agents through ACP in a coordinated pipeline, assigning each task to the agent best suited to perform it.

Take advantage of the IDE as more than a chat window by giving agents access to the project context, code understanding, developer-defined skills, and more!

By the end of the session, you’ll have a practical blueprint for building an agent that understands your development environment, follows your rules, and can coordinate other agents to complete real software-engineering tasks.','{"id": "a9a0ab98dd714b92adf0e055d6b2296f", "room": "TBA 2", "title": "Build the Builder: A Java Coding Agent in Your IDE with ACP", "endsAt": 1791370200000, "category": "[Conference, INTERMEDIATE, Agentic Engineering & Tooling]", "dayIndex": 3, "speakers": "[Vadim Briliantov]", "startsAt": 1791367200000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-10-07", "durationMinutes": 50, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-07T12:50:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-07T12:00:00"}'),
