0037498474,-0.0068092346,-0.027252197,-0.04144287,0.030792236,0.036254883,-0.026657104,0.028656006,-0.0040893555,-0.0043258667,0.007583618,0.018051147,0.051361084,0.014015198,0.006034851,0.00019323826,0.017486572,0.028549194,-0.017196655,0.00868988,0.0016498566,0.014595032,0.05331421,-0.015342712,0.03250122,-0.009002686,-0.04333496,0.028717041,0.0044403076,-0.019088745,-0.0029754639,0.0032291412,0.01977539,0.0087509155,-0.021865845,-0.0047836304,0.005619049,0.0135269165,0.006591797,0.007751465,0.001540184,0.057434082,0.0035572052,0.007843018,0.0034656525,0.024978638,-0.013061523,0.018081665,-0.009155273,0.011894226,-0.0026378632,0.038146973,-0.011619568,-0.001821518,0.04547119,0.018630981,-0.00076293945,0.0017375946,-0.013504028,-0.0048065186,0.00881958,-0.020858765,0.03274536,0.015289307,0.018508911,-0.004901886,0.036102295,0.0071525574,0.025924683,-0.041748047,0.021102905,-0.02532959,-0.0060195923,0.010871887,-0.03375244,-0.0059547424,0.018936157,-0.02192688,-0.037078857,-0.020767212,-0.012718201,-0.01763916,0.051513672,0.018173218,0.014144897,0.031402588,0.0031757355,-0.014404297,-0.01687622,-0.009025574,0.007286072,-0.007545471,-0.029525757,0.0021591187,0.009254456,-0.0284729,0.0070877075,0.02999878,0.0075683594,0.008003235,0.022445679,0.008285522,-0.017837524,-0.028686523,-0.020080566,-0.020736694]'::public.vector,'title:Life in the Ivory Tower: Confessions of a Former Developer Who Turned Into an Architect, description:Up until a couple of years ago, I was riding the waves of the latest technologies, shipping code, and mocking the "suits" who spent their days writing documentation that was never read. But then, the unthinkable happened. I joined a new organization and transitioned to the dark side. I became one of them: an Enterprise Architect. Or as I like to call myself: a fake enterprise architect.

I quickly found out that my new reality meant dealing with consultants pitching one-shot solutions as the ultimate silver bullet (pun intended), and uncovering the psychology of IT by learning why technical decisions are rarely about technology. At the same time, I wanted to stay in touch with the real world where frameworks evolve and pipelines run. But how on earth do you find the time for that?

This talk is a transparent look inside my shiny new office, commonly known as the Ivory Tower. Whether you''re a developer trying to challenge corporate IT decisions, an architect struggling to explain them, or you just want to make fun of enterprise architects (the real ones, that is): this session is for a broad audience, sharing lessons learned trying to bridge the gap between two worlds that should never be disconnected.

Expect to walk away with:

Tips on how to keep your technical skills alive while moving up the corporate ladder

A look into the psychology behind IT decision-making and why it''s never just about the code

How to foster real collaboration and break down the walls of the Ivory Tower','{"id": "7833831f54b94999bf1b9d2ba724f8dc", "room": "TBA 3", "title": "Life in the Ivory Tower: Confessions of a Former Developer Who Turned Into an Architect", "endsAt": 1791373500000, "category": "[Lunch Talk, INTERMEDIATE, Architecture & Modernization]", "dayIndex": 3, "speakers": "[Robin Custers]", "startsAt": 1791371100000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-10-07", "durationMinutes": 40, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-07T13:45:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-07T13:05:00"}'),
