408081,-0.00015556812,0.011734009,-0.0033950806,-0.024291992,0.021499634,-0.0067825317,-0.0087890625,-0.004512787,0.014007568,-0.0046958923,-0.049987793,0.023468018,0.014694214,-0.008377075,-0.009284973,0.0038528442,0.013580322,0.008811951,-0.018356323,0.0034255981,0.037872314,0.030838013,0.044708252,0.0022735596,0.022460938,-0.01449585,-0.0033359528,0.002981186,0.014831543,-0.037353516,-0.006038666,-0.016204834,-0.0011053085,0.0020561218,0.032409668,-0.025802612,0.044433594,-0.0034999847,0.025939941,0.022338867,0.010345459,0.060058594,0.028762817,0.030212402,0.009010315,0.051513672,-0.0395813,0.009208679,-0.022888184,0.003768921,-0.005138397,0.020477295,0.010848999,0.019485474,0.016311646,0.020690918,-0.0062942505,-0.003435135,-0.004009247,-0.002571106,-0.009277344,-0.054473877,0.046325684,-0.014434814,0.020690918,-0.028335571,0.014587402,0.02709961,0.03491211,-0.020553589,0.016143799,0.009025574,-0.0038604736,-0.0075035095,-0.0016345978,-0.0005311966,0.021057129,-0.04272461,-0.018814087,0.010116577,0.0001654625,-0.007183075,0.026504517,0.014282227,-0.029312134,0.012191772,-0.015380859,-0.03050232,0.0047302246,0.0021820068,0.013557434,-0.017150879,-0.006477356,-0.0052948,0.005874634,-0.01007843,-0.004009247,0.041381836,-0.04663086,0.008361816,0.009986877,0.00484848,-0.018936157,-0.008110046,-0.006919861,-0.012306213]'::public.vector,'title:Run Frontier Open Models on consumer hardware, description:Getting a model to run on a Mac is easy. Getting it to run fast — with production-grade throughput, stable memory behavior, and accuracy that survives optimization — is where the real engineering lives. This session goes deep into the inference stack behind the MLX ecosystem (mlx-vlm, mlx-audio, nativ — 5M+ downloads).
We''ll work through the full pipeline with measured numbers at every stage:
Unified memory in practice — why the architecture that makes 512GB of "VRAM" possible also changes how you batch, allocate, and evict; and where memory bandwidth, not compute, becomes the wall across M-series generations.
Continuous batching without a scheduler''s luxury — handling concurrent requests when the same RAM serves the OS, the app, and the model.
KV-cache compression and quantization — which techniques hold accuracy on real long-context workloads versus which only look good in benchmarks, with results from MATH500 and LongBench evaluations.
Speculative decoding across chip generations — when draft models pay off and when the overhead eats the win.
Porting war stories — bugs from porting 1000+ models, and the testing methodology that now catches them.
You''ll leave able to reason about the accuracy/latency/memory triangle on consumer hardware — and knowing which server-side habits to unlearn.
For engineers building or deploying on-device inference. Familiarity with transformer inference assumed.','{"id": "9d69af3b594d4863b2513ce2368e896a", "room": "TBA 2", "title": "Run Frontier Open Models on consumer hardware", "endsAt": 1791377400000, "category": "[Conference, INTERMEDIATE, Agentic Engineering & Tooling]", "dayIndex": 3, "speakers": "[Prince Canuma]", "startsAt": 1791374400000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-10-07", "durationMinutes": 50, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-07T14:50:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-07T14:00:00"}');
