01260376,0.010505676,-0.0020427704,-0.028289795,-0.02279663,0.0041923523,0.009544373,-0.022277832,0.04736328,0.0015087128,0.0038146973,-0.012428284,0.024658203,0.05569458,-0.004589081,0.0037631989,-0.0042152405,-0.002960205,0.013725281,0.0055656433,0.0025844574,0.00667572,-0.0012750626,0.05593872,0.005203247,0.015068054,-0.03137207,-0.03475952,0.042816162,0.011962891,-0.023406982,0.005290985,0.014472961,0.00081825256,0.013519287,-0.01335907,-0.011169434,0.035583496,0.0032196045,0.016204834,0.0042037964,-0.0046958923,0.0914917,-0.003856659,0.012275696,0.019760132,0.010505676,-0.031555176,0.033050537,-0.018798828,0.002981186,-0.015090942,0.04522705,0.021728516,0.009300232,0.02130127,0.021377563,0.011116028,-0.0019006729,-0.024505615,-0.0044288635,-0.015556335,-0.026428223,0.033721924,0.005695343,0.0061683655,-0.0035037994,0.027786255,0.012771606,0.010368347,-0.030273438,-0.0014696121,-0.0013122559,-0.007003784,0.0015029907,-0.013969421,-0.018325806,-0.012062073,-0.012664795,-0.042938232,-0.0053253174,-0.0013036728,-0.009101868,0.02973938,0.0036258698,0.010116577,0.025558472,0.029281616,0.0046081543,-0.03378296,0.000705719,-0.030548096,-0.02381897,-0.017868042,0.0017347336,0.00868988,-0.025222778,-0.0066337585,0.024459839,-0.013648987,-0.017578125,0.0287323,-0.013442993,-0.015716553,-0.04397583,-0.009284973,-0.035339355]'::public.vector,'title:Integration patterns for distributed systems, description:Today''s applications are interconnected: they expose APIs, publish events, call third-party services, and externalize states. They must therefore address the fundamental challenges of distributed systems, such as out-of-order delivery, retries, idempotence, and partial failures. To address these challenges, architects must carefully evaluate integration options while considering how to manage complex system interactions. In this session, learn about common design trade-offs for distributed systems and how to navigate them with design patterns, illustrated by examples.

The target audience is from many floors the architect elevator would stop by: Software architects, software developers, IT executives and everyone around - such general software architecture sessions are relevant for many roles in every company in every segment. For those with legacy architectures, it provides an outlook on what migration and modernization transformation they could go through in order to get towards flexible, individually evolvable and scalable systems that are loosely coupled.','{"id": "75db05246e474b8dafffa7b70ff7580a", "room": "TBA 2", "title": "Integration patterns for distributed systems", "endsAt": 1791381600000, "category": "[Conference, ADVANCED, Architecture & Modernization]", "dayIndex": 3, "speakers": "[Dirk Fröhner, Sabarish Sekar]", "startsAt": 1791378600000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-10-07", "durationMinutes": 50, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-07T16:00:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-07T15:10:00"}'),
