With AI now entering the picture, many hope it will fill the gaps. It won’t.

I’ll show the simple ideas that change everything: how to think about documentation in the right levels, how to keep it close to the code, and how to avoid the decay we all know too well. These are the decisions that have to be made before any tool or process can work.

The documentation won’t write itself. But there are ways to make it far easier and far more reliable.

Key take-aways:

• How to separate architecture, API, and code-level documentation
• What “docs as code” means once the slogans are gone
• Why missing context stays missing—with or without AI','{"id": "fe2996c2c76349d3a7f7c46739d916b1", "room": "TBA 5", "title": "Why Documentation Won’t Write Itself—and AI Won’t Save You", "endsAt": 1791381600000, "category": "[Conference, BEGINNER, Development Practices]", "dayIndex": 3, "speakers": "[Christian Heitzmann]", "startsAt": 1791378600000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-10-07", "durationMinutes": 50, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-07T16:00:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-07T15:10:00"}'),
