3369,0.005382538,-0.0115356445,-0.028793335,0.017913818,0.014816284,-0.02230835,0.01939392,-0.008834839,-0.00844574,-0.0007452965,0.024627686,0.028427124,-0.0042762756,0.012542725,0.0023555756,-0.020568848,-0.008964539,-0.022705078,0.027404785,0.0040664673,0.003326416,0.046569824,-0.00983429,0.0010795593,-0.015556335,-0.010551453,0.01838684,-0.00067806244,-0.013404846,-0.037597656,0.010665894,0.036834717,-0.009216309,-0.010681152,-0.0446167,0.029632568,0.018218994,-0.004764557,0.014015198,-0.012741089,0.08105469,-0.0012369156,-0.0063476562,0.0046958923,0.043151855,-0.04348755,0.024459839,-0.002117157,0.041625977,0.00038552284,0.019226074,0.007926941,0.0058403015,0.003042221,0.019012451,-0.0026435852,-0.032806396,-0.014976501,-0.020523071,0.011436462,-0.024902344,0.040985107,0.009666443,0.010749817,-0.019561768,0.0072746277,0.020462036,0.019134521,-0.0071983337,0.020141602,0.0023708344,-0.004627228,-0.0056991577,0.00995636,-0.0015220642,0.0154800415,-0.033721924,-0.027404785,-0.013069153,-0.0022773743,-0.014137268,0.038391113,0.025299072,0.0069465637,0.013923645,0.028945923,-0.010398865,-0.03201294,-0.00015890598,-0.020141602,-0.035827637,-0.014328003,-0.021697998,0.021255493,-0.025802612,-0.041656494,0.021453857,-0.038482666,0.011856079,0.023208618,-0.00374794,-0.00945282,-0.032409668,-0.008972168,-0.04168701]'::public.vector,'title:Building, Shipping and Governing AI Agents on Kubernetes, description:An engineering agent that checks GitHub pull requests sounds simple: find the open PRs, check their status and report which ones are ready to merge. But when I connected one to GitHub''s MCP server, it was provided 44 tools, including seventeen that could modify the repository. Checking which open pull requests were ready to merge took nineteen tool calls and moved around 75,000 bytes of raw API responses to produce a nine-line answer.

In this live demo, we''ll build and deploy an agent on Kubernetes using three open source projects (KAgent, AgentGateway, AgentRegistry), then show how to make it safer and more efficient through platform configuration rather than changes to the agent code.

What you will learn:

Build and ship: Use agentregistry to scaffold an agent from approved components and deploy it with kagent as a managed Kubernetes workload.

Cut the overhead: Use agentgateway to reduce nineteen tool calls to two and keep intermediate API responses out of the model''s context.

Control access: Keep credentials at the gateway and apply permissions per agent, so a read-only agent is only given the tools it actually needs.','{"id": "46ba662ba78b458b8b7076e2feeeacec", "room": "TBA 2", "title": "Building, Shipping and Governing AI Agents on Kubernetes", "endsAt": 1791463200000, "category": "[Conference, INTERMEDIATE, Agentic Engineering & Tooling]", "dayIndex": 4, "speakers": "[Tom O''Rourke, Ep Heijting]", "startsAt": 1791460200000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-10-08", "durationMinutes": 50, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-08T14:40:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-08T13:50:00"}'),
