Most people are unaware of the hassles we face (or inflict upon ourselves) regarding legacy systems, technical choices, or our own organizational structures.

The goal is to have a laugh about our points of contention (Lombok? Reactive? Date formats?), our nemeses (printers, CORS), language quirks—including the fact that you can run *Doom* inside the TypeScript type system—why no one truly understands C++, wacky HTTP status codes, centering `div`s (of course), the Y2K non-event versus the real Y2K38 problem, RGB vs. CMYK, April Fools'' RFCs, web page and file encoding issues, consistently botched Excel integrations, business-dictated file naming conventions, the headache of IEEE 754 rounding, the "ultimate" email validation regex, hidden (or missing) CPU instructions, the shift toward inclusivity since 2020 (dropping "master/slave" terminology...), SOA, the long-awaited WASM, corporate timesheets, and—last but not least—the crucial question: *how do you exit vi?*','{"id": "0ab4e7bd145b4cd99530cba8b984a180", "room": "TBA 7", "title": "The Dark Secrets of Computer Science", "endsAt": 1791467400000, "category": "[Conference, INTERMEDIATE, Mind the Geek]", "dayIndex": 4, "speakers": "[Fabien Marsaud]", "startsAt": 1791464400000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-10-08", "durationMinutes": 50, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-08T15:50:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-08T15:00:00"}');
