8598328,0.00522995,-0.0010566711,-0.0015134811,-0.050048828,0.016479492,0.0036144257,-0.008857727,0.033599854,-0.022491455,-0.011360168,-0.018249512,0.009361267,0.054870605,0.008224487,0.022918701,-0.028320312,-0.015716553,0.010307312,-0.00598526,-0.026260376,0.0016403198,-0.003786087,0.015541077,0.004234314,0.0070114136,-0.011978149,-0.028945923,0.031433105,0.022018433,-0.03994751,0.01084137,-0.013664246,-0.0073127747,-0.0113220215,-0.014389038,-0.027938843,0.040161133,0.016571045,0.03451538,-0.022369385,-0.0015182495,0.0440979,-0.014053345,0.012863159,0.025131226,0.009895325,-0.029556274,0.036132812,-0.011528015,0.014945984,-0.007686615,0.03677368,0.0024604797,-0.00034499168,0.005996704,0.0061531067,-0.00223732,-0.030853271,-0.012397766,0.0023727417,0.008155823,0.0064811707,0.057159424,0.016677856,0.035858154,-0.02267456,0.029953003,0.023986816,0.00995636,-0.017623901,0.029586792,-0.014053345,-0.016418457,-0.009506226,-0.006717682,-0.013214111,-0.0023880005,-0.015426636,0.0057792664,-0.04486084,-0.0025844574,0.011482239,0.025039673,0.01739502,0.0027122498,0.0289917,0.009475708,-0.028549194,-0.009681702,-0.0034236908,-0.008468628,-0.014053345,0.0007109642,-0.020385742,0.011314392,-0.017913818,-0.015899658,0.04812622,-0.040283203,0.015899658,0.033203125,0.0019702911,-0.017166138,-0.037963867,0.014511108,-0.029159546]'::public.vector,'title:Production-Ready Java Containers: Smaller, Faster, Smarter, description:Java runs exceptionally well in containers, but simply putting a JAR into a container image leaves a lot of optimization opportunities on the table.

In this session, we’ll explore practical techniques for building leaner and more efficient Java containers. We’ll look at using jlink to create tailored Java runtimes, techniques such as CDS and AOT to improve startup and warmup, JVM container awareness and memory configuration, and container-building practices that reduce image size and improve deployment.

But smaller isn’t always better. Every optimization comes with trade-offs involving startup time, memory consumption, throughput, observability, security, and maintainability.

Using a Java application as our starting point, we’ll progressively optimize its container and measure what actually changes. The goal is not to produce the smallest possible image, but to understand the tools available and make informed engineering decisions about what a better Java container means for your workload.','{"id": "9c0c782532134e9c857b2574fa23f0b4", "room": "TBA 9", "title": "Production-Ready Java Containers: Smaller, Faster, Smarter", "endsAt": 1791477000000, "category": "[Conference, BEGINNER, Java Language & Platform]", "dayIndex": 4, "speakers": "[Albert Attard]", "startsAt": 1791474000000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-10-08", "durationMinutes": 50, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-08T18:30:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-08T17:40:00"}'),
