67737,-0.008026123,0.032958984,-0.020462036,-0.01210022,-0.034057617,-0.0036144257,0.029205322,0.007774353,0.021057129,-0.010482788,-0.001159668,-0.009681702,0.011047363,0.024841309,0.023147583,0.01096344,-0.008522034,-0.0019607544,0.0018720627,-0.030166626,-0.021759033,0.022460938,0.0018253326,0.01651001,0.0030918121,-0.0022525787,-0.013038635,-0.017913818,0.02810669,-0.0038089752,-0.03640747,0.041168213,-0.008850098,0.02154541,0.0026931763,0.011131287,-0.021011353,0.041015625,0.02015686,0.019302368,0.025100708,-0.0085372925,-0.0050964355,-0.0010213852,-0.00069999695,0.0049438477,0.01512146,-0.01234436,0.020004272,0.0029582977,0.028411865,-0.023742676,0.015083313,-0.0029201508,0.02104187,-0.0017461777,0.00969696,-0.0026474,0.02935791,-0.01928711,0.008850098,0.027923584,0.016464233,0.028137207,0.01802063,0.002904892,0.0028514862,0.009918213,-0.00065517426,0.009437561,-0.019119263,0.00064849854,0.0056381226,-0.0049552917,0.0077590942,-0.0025939941,0.029388428,-0.028549194,-0.017196655,0.0058250427,-0.04940796,-0.007446289,-0.021865845,0.016967773,-0.0065231323,-0.016418457,0.027282715,-0.0109939575,-0.0051078796,-0.023101807,-0.0016813278,-0.018463135,-0.020889282,-0.012741089,-0.002855301,-0.006378174,-0.018310547,-0.030929565,0.028121948,-0.028320312,0.038085938,0.022094727,-0.028915405,-0.0158844,-0.025878906,0.010604858,-0.045074463]'::public.vector,'title:Escaping Java''s Allocation Gravity with Value Classes, description:Java 28 will soon introduce, as a preview feature, value classes (JEP 401), a new kind of class that renounces its identity to get better performance.

In this presentation, we will explain what value classes are, how the Java spec was re-interpreted to conjure their existence, how they interact with existing code and what optimizations they enable both on the stack and the heap.

To make it concrete, we''ll explore these ideas through the simulation and rendering of a black hole: calculating the trajectories of thousands of light rays through curved spacetime means manipulating huge numbers of vectors, rays, coordinates, and intermediate values.

Finally, we will look ahead at the experimental "!" syntax for null-restricted types, its optimization potential, and the limitations of the current design.

Join us to discover value classes and the current state of Project Valhalla.','{"id": "b307e127cba74019a655b72b9444a836", "room": "TBA 3", "title": "Escaping Java''s Allocation Gravity with Value Classes", "endsAt": 1791534000000, "category": "[Conference, ADVANCED, Java Language & Platform]", "dayIndex": 5, "speakers": "[Clément de Tastes, Remi Forax]", "startsAt": 1791531000000, "phaseOfDay": "MORNING", "conferenceDay": "2026-10-09", "durationMinutes": 50, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-09T10:20:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-09T09:30:00"}');
