24,-0.024093628,0.007858276,-0.013130188,-0.034210205,-0.02418518,0.008041382,0.031311035,-0.032592773,0.020309448,-0.0005469322,-0.0068969727,0.019256592,-0.002746582,0.056518555,0.021759033,-0.0028839111,-0.009864807,-0.0006585121,0.047790527,-0.026107788,-0.02053833,-0.003742218,0.026611328,0.043426514,-0.0070762634,0.04348755,-0.022399902,-0.006980896,0.040374756,0.0051994324,0.011047363,0.020645142,0.02331543,0.027435303,-0.020446777,-0.010955811,-0.027862549,-0.011734009,0.0030822754,0.017044067,-0.01928711,0.0060806274,0.044830322,0.012184143,-0.01361084,-0.004737854,0.026550293,-0.037200928,0.0071754456,-0.0018787384,0.007133484,-0.0076446533,0.034118652,-0.0035305023,-0.0066871643,0.0064086914,0.008804321,-0.00069999695,0.015914917,-0.003627777,0.010231018,0.010894775,-0.0015773773,0.027038574,0.03857422,0.045715332,0.008735657,0.020629883,0.008636475,0.021530151,0.004749298,0.006614685,-0.022262573,-0.02607727,0.0284729,-0.021118164,0.021057129,0.02709961,-0.022064209,-0.04046631,-0.025238037,-0.009925842,-0.01348114,0.016067505,0.0048980713,-0.002008438,-0.0002272129,-0.030227661,-0.035858154,-0.022903442,-0.0028877258,0.0068969727,-0.008071899,-0.016464233,-0.0066452026,0.029434204,-0.019317627,-0.007396698,0.0112838745,-0.024612427,0.03286743,0.012290955,0.027191162,-0.02381897,-0.031463623,0.0061302185,-0.01309967]'::public.vector,'title:Is AI killing XP practices? (Or are we letting it?), description:For the last 20-30 years, the software industry has been learning — sometimes the hard way — how to build better software. Practices like TDD, refactoring, pair programming, simple design, collective code ownership, continuous integration, and coding standards didn''t appear by magic. They came from pain, failed projects, and the brilliant minds behind XP — Kent Beck, Ward Cunningham, and Ron Jeffries — who asked: "can we do this better?"

And now? Now we have AI. And suddenly, some developers are asking: "do we still need all that?"

In this session, we''ll look at 7 XP practices that are genuinely at risk in an AI-assisted world — not because they''re wrong, but because AI makes it tempting to skip them. We''ll see what happens when teams do skip them (spoiler: it''s not pretty 😅), and more importantly, how these practices don''t just survive the AI era — they become *more critical than ever*.

AI amplifies everything. Good habits and bad ones. If your team has no tests, no shared standards, no feedback loop — AI will help you build the mess of your life, faster than ever before.

AI is the best stress test XP practices have ever had. Spoiler: they pass 😉','{"id": "afed8c8547674bc4bd5ff6b14c00d73c", "room": "TBA 3", "title": "Is AI killing XP practices? (Or are we letting it?)", "endsAt": 1791538200000, "category": "[Conference, INTERMEDIATE, Development Practices]", "dayIndex": 5, "speakers": "[Nacho Cougil]", "startsAt": 1791535200000, "phaseOfDay": "MORNING", "conferenceDay": "2026-10-09", "durationMinutes": 50, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-09T11:30:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-09T10:40:00"}'),
