Fifty years later, we took our trains out of the attic with a big idea in mind: to convert them to digital. With some Seeed Studio, Bluetooth, and TinyGo, we managed to get a functional railway network, were we can manage speed, direction, and lights of each train individually.

This talk speaks about hardware and software architecture for embedded systems, in a retrospective format about all our successes (and mistakes) during the conception of this project. Everything is open-source, so don''t hesitate to use it, fork it, and enhance it!','{"id": "68e3c8416eae4cd9b6cb739b3634db30", "room": "TBA 4", "title": "My old trains have a second life, with TinyGo!", "endsAt": 1791542400000, "category": "[Conference, BEGINNER, Mind the Geek]", "dayIndex": 5, "speakers": "[Florian FORESTIER, Justine Bachelard]", "startsAt": 1791539400000, "phaseOfDay": "MORNING", "conferenceDay": "2026-10-09", "durationMinutes": 50, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-09T12:40:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-10-09T11:50:00"}'),
